-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# pin28-variable-resolved-source-loads (`28K` §1 rul-unloadable-is-unlicensed, the RICHNESS half;
# `28K` §10 bitem9) — a load target built through resolvable variable-flow is analyzed exactly as
# a literal path is. The spelling is the one `28K` §1 names: "a source path through resolvable
# variable-flow (`LIB=./oracles; . "$LIB/yum.sh"`) loads fine, exactly as `"$CERTS"` has always
# resolved".
#
# THE OPERAND CARRIES `./`, and that is the whole correction (`30Ib`'s own named fix, filed at
# `30Mb` §4). This case used to spell `. "$PKG.oracle.sh"` — SLASH-LESS, which is a `PATH` search
# under `30I:rul-dot-resolves-as-sh` and resolves nowhere at v0 whatever the variable holds. So the
# case's parity claim was true of two NON-resolutions and could not have caught a variable-flow
# regression: the literal twin it compared against was equally unresolved. With `./` the operand is
# a real path, the target really resolves, and the comparison is between two spellings that both
# reach the same file.
#
# WHAT THIS PINS, precisely: the target resolves through `SourceLiteralPlane` — the same one-word
# window every other operand already used — so there is exactly ONE resolver and the variable form
# cannot drift from the literal form. Nothing in `funcenv` knows about the variable spelling at all.
#
# AND IT IS OBSERVABLE, which is the point of the correction: the `.` resolves, so
# `foobar__is_converged` really binds at that line and site 2 ships a real verdict-lane check. Under
# the slash-less spelling the operand resolved nowhere, the load HAVOC'd the environment, and every
# site read `unresolvable-no-probe` — the same output a broken resolver would have produced, which
# is why the old shape could not have caught one.
#
# NON-VACUOUS, and gate-3 is what makes it so: before bitem9 the parser ⊤-rejected this line as
# `DynamicExecution`, so the book carried TWO undeclared error-severity diagnostics
# (`syntax-unsupported` + `cfg-top-node`) and gate-3 would have failed this case outright. Clean
# stderr here is the whole difference.
#
# THE APPLY STILL RUNS THE LINE, so nobody misreads a shipped probe as an elision: this case commits
# no site records, so nothing is measured and the site runs (`kFAIL-perform`). What the sites ABOVE
# it read is `28O:res-book-sourcing-walls-at-the-site` — a top-level `.` is an unmodeled command in
# book position and walls (`opaque-poison-is-the-product`) — whose blessing question
# (`.`-of-a-proven-load-inert-file) is routed to the human alongside `command -v`.
#
# NO `mocks/`, deliberately: `.` is a SPECIAL builtin, so a non-interactive shell that cannot find
# what it sources EXITS, and the exec rail's cwd is an empty throwaway sandbox. That is why no
# executing corpus case has ever carried a top-level `.`, and it is `28K` §8
# `res-book-ships-its-load-closure` — named there, unbuilt.
PKG=foobar
. "./$PKG.oracle.sh"
foobar sync-certs /etc/nginx/certs

-- foobar.oracle.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
foobar__is_converged() {
   verb="$1"; shift
   case "$verb" in
   sync-certs) foobar status --certs-current -- "$1" ;;
   *) return 2 ;;
   esac
}

-- probe-results.txt --
# Nothing is probed (see the book's header), so this stream carries no site records.

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --pre-source foobar.oracle.sh --results - < probe-results.txt
cfg: warning[cfg-errexit-unknown]: Dorc could not tell whether `set -e` is in
effect at one or more commands (a non-literal `set` argument, or paths that
disagree), so it plans as though each might abort the book on failure -- the
cautious reading; it can cost elisions, never correctness.
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=eef693c08d1513f3d0f1941b724cebbf4f50dcff87e6bd33a2a7282eda62c134 sites=1 @@dorc@@\n'
# site 2: dorc-auto:foobar@converged
foobar__is_converged() {
   verb="$1"; shift
   case "$verb" in
   sync-certs) foobar status --certs-current -- "$1" ;;
   *) return 2 ;;
   esac
}
foobar__is_converged 'sync-certs' '/etc/nginx/certs'; _rc=$?; if [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ]; then _e=holds; elif [ "$_rc" -eq 1 ]; then _e=absent; else _e=cant-tell; fi; printf 'dorc site 2 effect=%s rc=%s @@dorc@@\n' "$_e" "$_rc"
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 1 site run unprobed (no read-only check could
be shipped): `. "./$PKG.oracle.sh"` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site detail
(the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:42:1
   42 | . "./$PKG.oracle.sh"
      | \__________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `. "./$PKG.oracle.sh"`
hint: '.' (line 42) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
emission: note[plan-import-rewritten]: [unwritten: plan-import-rewritten]
      [ book.sh:42:1
   42 | . "./$PKG.oracle.sh"
      | \__________________/
